{
  "$comment": "Every operation both SDKs wrap, keyed by METHOD + path exactly as openapi.json spells them. `operation_id` is the backend's (FastAPI generates some; never shown to users). `name` is the stable SDK operation name the generators key on: each language derives its method and type names from it. `class` decides what may be retried (retry-policy.json): `safe` - free, and a repeat changes nothing the first call did not (every GET, run and enrichment job cancel, credit quote, list rename, list item add and remove, feedback vote, and the CSV exports, which are free; a repeated export that becomes a background job starts a second job, which is harmless and expires); `billed` - charged after the work succeeds, as the last step, so a failure before it costs nothing; a first page of search or similar opens a new paid session, so a request that may have been sent is never repeated; `keyed` - the server reads Idempotency-Key, so a repeat with the same key is recognised (agent run start, enrichment job start and lookup, feedback); `once` - free, but a repeat is not the same as one call (a second list create answers 409 lists.name_taken, a second delete 404). `idempotency` is how the server treats Idempotency-Key (`required`, `optional` or `none`). The SDK sends one wherever it is not `none` (the `keyed` routes): one key per call, generated unless the caller passes one, and reused on that call's retries. `response` is the success body: `json`, `empty` (204), `sse` (text/event-stream), `csv`, or `csv_or_job` (200 text/csv for up to the server's sync row limit, else 202 JSON ExportJobView to poll at /v1/exports/{job_id}). `paging`, where present: `cursor` style sends `cursor` in the body or query and reads `next_cursor`, items under `items`; a page repeats the first request's other fields unchanged, and `binds_limit` means the cursor is also bound to `limit`, so the page size must not change between pages (POST /v1/relationships/reach); `page_number` style sends `page` and reads `has_more`. /v1/relationships/ask pages whichever envelope answered and is handled by each SDK by hand. A test fails if an operation in openapi.json is neither listed here nor under `not_wrapped`, which lists the operations no SDK wraps, each with its `reason`.",
